// tests/kernel/drivers/wifi/hw_smoke_regdom_placeholder.pdx — R38.M6-004 (#1317)
//
// DORMANT hardware-gated placeholder for the regulatory-domain
// enforcement smoke on a real ThinkPad T14 G4.
//
// #1317 delivers `tools/hw-smoke-r38-regdom.md`: an operator recipe
// for asserting that the channel-set enforced by net80211_regdom
// after regdom_loader publishes each region matches what the AX211
// radio was configured for.  Per design/roadmap/next-wave-synthesis.md
// §10 D7 (gated:hardware discipline), an issue whose acceptance
// requires PHYSICAL hardware in the loop CANNOT emit a golden
// fingerprint from a QEMU-OVMF boot -- QEMU has no CNVi transport,
// no radio EEPROM to read, and no GNSS receiver to poll, and the
// fingerprint would lie.
//
// The RIGHT SHAPE for a dormant issue is a placeholder module that
// COMPILES but emits NOTHING.  This module is that placeholder.  It
// carries no witness function, no fingerprint, and no boot-witness
// registration.  The build-time coverage gate is satisfied because
// there is nothing to cover.
//
// When R38.M6 or a later milestone lands the regdom-enforcement
// smoke on real hardware, this file is replaced by a real synth
// that emits "REGDOM HW-SMOKE OK" and takes ITS marker into the
// golden.
//
// The empty module is deliberately unreferenced from
// r30_platform.pdx.  That is the whole point of the dormant shape:
// nothing at boot calls into it, so nothing at boot lies about it.

module HwSmokeRegdomPlaceholder = structure {
  // Compile-only marker so paideia-as accepts an "empty" module.
  pub let _r38_m6_004_hw_smoke_regdom_placeholder : u64 = 0
}
